; A phase follower, with its variational equations: an oscillator (c, s),
; d/dt c = -(om * s) and d/dt s = om * c, whose angle atan2(s, c) a follower
; phi chases, d/dt phi = k * (atan2(s, c) - phi), beside two states that
; each hold one of the unary inverse functions, d/dt q = asin(q / 2) -
; atan(q) and d/dt v = acos(v / 2) - v (written q * 0.5 and v * 0.5: a
; product, so that the primal step holds no division), with one tangent
; vector stepped beside the state by the same program. All four of the math
; library's inverse routines are in its step - atan2 of two state
; components, both differentiated, and atan, asin and acos each of a state
; component - and their rules in the tangent (M4's build; the study's
; section 7, docs/studies/M4-invtrig.md): the quotients
; da / fma(a, a, 1), da / sqrt(fma(-a, a, 1)), da / (-sqrt(fma(-a, a, 1)))
; and fma(b, da, -(a * db)) / fma(b, b, a * a), none of which writes a
; routine node, so each costs a vector one run-time division. It is
; compiled round rdn, every rounded operation toward negative infinity,
; where acos's rule, whose negation is taken of the root before the
; quotient rounds, is another value as well as other bytes than the
; negation taken after, and atan2's likewise. The run keeps the oscillator
; in the half plane c > 0 (a box of lanes, and om T far below pi), so that
; atan2's primal, which jumps on the negative axis, does not, and q and v
; inside [-2, 2], asin's and acos's domain.
system phasefollower
format fp64
round  rdn
state  c, s, phi, q, v
tangent w
param  om = 1/2, k = 1/4
d/dt c = -(om * s)
d/dt s = om * c
d/dt phi = k * (atan2(s, c) - phi)
d/dt q = asin(q * 0.5) - atan(q)
d/dt v = acos(v * 0.5) - v
step   rk4, h = 1/64
